From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: djvalic <djvalic@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 26 Nov 2026 12:19:23 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] settings: an Advanced settings switch shows every row or only
 the everyday ones

A switch at the bottom of the Settings root, in its own card: Advanced
settings.
Off, the rows outside the everyday set hide: Touch & Input, Purge Box,
Updates and Plugins on the root; the Printer page's model row and Manage
Printers; Machine Limits, Retraction Settings, Multi-Filament System
Management, Z Offset by Material, Allow cold load/unload, Number slots
from 0, Macro Buttons and Apply start options to prints from other apps
on Printing; Sensors, IFS firmware and Power Devices on Devices; Cancel
Escalation and its timeout, Confirm before running macros and the
nozzle-hit guard's Sensitivity on Safety & Alerts; Printers, Host, API
Key and Screen Sharing on Connection; Performance, Storage, Firmware
Update, Share Usage Data, View Telemetry Data, Log Level, Log detail,
Reset root password, Boot into recovery, Return to Flashforge firmware
and Extended memory on System; Upload Debug Bundle, Discord Community,
Getting Started and User Guide on Help & About.

The setting is a PersistedSetting of SystemSettingsManager
(/advanced_settings, subject settings_advanced). It defaults on, so a
settings.json from before it keeps every row; Kiln OS's first-install
seed stores false, and get_default_config() with the user preferences,
which Config::reset_to_defaults() uses, stores false, so a factory reset
starts with it off.

Each hidden row or container carries a bind_flag_if_eq on
settings_advanced. Bindings on one flag compose (lv_xml_bind_compose.c),
so the rows' own gates keep working in both modes, and no wrapper sits
at height 0 under a card when a row's own gate hides it. The bindings
follow the subject, so pages already built change at once.
double_bind_widgets.inc is regenerated, which also adds four Kiln pairs
it lacked. English, English (UK, empty) and Russian strings.
